<!--
  Codivio sitemap (Phase 3.1; updated for the Phase 4.9 SEO follow-up,
  Phase 3.18 HTML Site Map, and Phase 3.18's XSLT visual-design follow-up).

  The <?xml-stylesheet?> line above is presentation-only: a browser that
  opens this URL directly renders it via public/sitemap.xsl + sitemap.css
  (Codivio-branded HTML view). Search engines ignore this instruction and
  read the urlset/url structure below exactly as before — the file
  remains valid XML per the sitemaps.org schema, unchanged in every way
  that matters for crawling. See public/sitemap.xsl's own header comment
  for why the styling is a linked file rather than inline (CSP).

  Lists only real, currently indexable public pages. As of the Phase 4.9
  follow-up, 2 of the 34 tool pages — QR Code Generator and QR Code
  Scanner — have shipped real functionality (see shared/seo/tools.ts,
  whose robots entries for these two flipped to index,follow) and are
  listed below. The remaining 32 tool pages stay excluded: every one of
  them is still noindex today because ToolPage.tsx renders a "coming soon"
  placeholder, not real functionality; a sitemap should only list pages
  meant to be indexed. Update this file again when another tool ships real
  functionality and its shared/seo/tools.ts robots entry flips to
  index,follow.

  Phase 3.18 adds /sitemap itself — the real, index,follow, human-readable
  HTML directory of every page/tool (see shared/seo/pages.ts's PAGE_SEO.sitemap
  and src/App.tsx's SitemapPage) — at the same priority tier as /faq and
  /pricing (0.5/monthly): a useful secondary navigation page, not core like
  the homepage or the tools hub.

  The Human-Readable Robots Policy Page follow-up adds /robots the same
  way — the real, index,follow HTML explanation of robots.txt (see
  shared/seo/pages.ts's PAGE_SEO.robots and src/App.tsx's RobotsPage) — at
  the same 0.5/monthly tier as /sitemap. This does not touch robots.txt
  itself in any way; it only lists the new /robots explainer page here,
  exactly like every other real PAGE_SEO entry must be (see the existing
  test enforcing that below).

  Static (not worker-generated) for now, matching robots.txt/llms.txt —
  see DECISIONS.md's "SEO Metadata Architecture" entry for why a dynamic,
  D1-driven sitemap is future work rather than being built here.

  SEO Sitemap Completion audit (2026-09-22): added a lastmod date to every
  URL. Each value is the real last-changed date of that page's actual
  content source, taken from `git log`'s commit date for the relevant file
  (a pickaxe search on the page's own component function for the pages
  defined inside src/App.tsx, or the file's own last-commit date for a
  standalone file) — never a fabricated "today" date. Update the relevant
  lastmod field by hand whenever a page's real content changes, the same
  way priority/changefreq are already hand-maintained.

  Follow-up correction (2026-10-19): the original audit's pickaxe method
  missed body-only edits (it only detects a function's own signature line
  changing) and shared-component dependencies (e.g. /privacy, /terms, and
  /cookies all render through the shared LegalPage component, not just
  their own wrapper function). Re-verified every lastmod against real git
  history with this gap accounted for; 5 of 14 values were stale and have
  been corrected here. /blog's changefreq stays monthly, but its original
  rationale ("a static, hand-written preview list — no CMS exists yet")
  is itself now stale: Phase 8.5 replaced that static list with real,
  D1-backed, Admin-published content. monthly is kept as a reasonable
  default, not because it has been empirically validated — only one real
  article has ever been published, which is not enough data to establish
  an actual cadence. Revisit this value once real publishing frequency is
  known.
-->
